<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="luteolin glycosides biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6239" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. However, there is some evidence in the literature that this pathway is specialized to species that do not include Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Flavonoids are widely distributed in the plant kingdom. Flavonoids are a highly diverse class of plant secondary metabolites (with about 9000 compounds identified so far |CITS: [16137727]|) derived from the phenylpropanoid pathway. Based on core structures and chemical modifications flavonoids are classified as chalcones, aurones, flavonols, flavones, flavanones, isoflavones and anthocyanins. Flavonoids serve as pigments (red and purple anthocyanin pigments), act as signals for pollinators and other organisms, participate in plant hormone signaling, facilitate pollen-tube growth, protect plants from UV-B radiation, and function as phytoalexins |CITS: [11130659]|, |CITS: [10924197]|, |CITS: [11402179]|. Flavone and flavonol-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycosides make up one of the largest classes of flavonoid constituents with over 2000 known structures. Although |FRAME:GLC| |CITS:[5058406]| and |FRAME:CPD0-1112| |CITS:[1939145]| are the most frequent &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycoside conjugates found on flavones and flavonols, the presence of arabinose, xylose, and glucuronic acid has also been reported. The formation of |FRAME:CPD-14825| from |FRAME:LUTEOLIN-7-O-BETA-D-GLUCOSIDE| catalyzed by a flavonoid 1,6-glucosyltransferase isolated from cultured cells of |FRAME:TAX-4058| |CITS:[19561332]| |CITS:[17940060]| further extends the structural complexity of luteolin glycosides occurring in plants. |FRAME:5734-TETRAHYDROXYFLAVONE Luteolin| and glycosidic derivatives thereof belong to the more common flavones found in plants. Several potential functions have been ascribed to luteolin and its glycosides. In humans these compounds have been shown to exhibit anti-oxidant |CITS: [15543940]|, |CITS: [16732514]|, and anti-inflammatory activities |CITS: [17977562]|.</Description>
