    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-17875" textLabel="flavonol 7-O-rhamnosyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9LNE6" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup, O-28, hartmut">Utilizing transcriptome coexpression analysis comparing the correlation of the expression of known flavonoid biosynthetic genes with candidate genes a potential glycosyltransferase gene was identified in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. The gene (UGT89C1/At1g06000) encoded a flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase which exclusively rhamnosylated the 7-position of flavonol mono- and diglucosides and could only utilize |FRAME:UDP-L-RHAMNOSE UDP-&amp;beta;-L-rhamnose| as sugar donor. The molecular function of the flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase was confirmed through T-DNA mutants with an transcriptional blocked UGT89C1. The metabolic profile of the knock-out mutant lacked all typical rhamnosylated compounds usually dominating the |FRAME:TAX-3701 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;| wildtyp. The full length cDNA encoding the flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase was recombinant expressed in |FRAME:TAX-562 &lt;i&gt;Escherichia coli&lt;/i&gt;| and characterized in terms of catalytic properties. The flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase was able to catalyze the formation of |FRAME:CPD1F-462 kaempferol-3-rhamnoside-7-rhamnoside| from |FRAME:CPD1F-461 kaempferol-3-rhamnoside|, |FRAME:CPD1F-420 kaempferol-3-glucoside-7-rhamnoside| from |FRAME:CPD1F-453 kaempferol-3-glucoside|, |FRAME:CPD1F-431 kaempferol-3-O-gentiobioside-7-O-rhamnoside| from |FRAME:CPD-14847 kaempferol-3-gentiobioside| and |FRAME:CPD-15023 kaempferol 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyl(1-&gt;2)glucoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside| from |FRAME:CPD-8067 kaempferol 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-&amp;beta;-&lt;i&gt;D&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyl-(1-&gt;2)-glucoside|. When |FRAME:CPD-520 quercetin| mono- and diglucosides were used as substrates the flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase converted with lower efficiency |FRAME:CPD-8013 quercetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside| to |FRAME:CPDQT-26 quercetin-3-rhamnoside-7-rhamnoside| and with high efficiency |FRAME:CPD1F-437 quercetin-3-glucoside| to |FRAME:CPD-5521 quercetin-3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside|, |FRAME:CPD-14849 quercetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-sophoroside| to |FRAME:CPD-15021 quercetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyl(1-&gt;2)glucoside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside| and |FRAME:CPD-14820 quercetin-3-gentiobioside| to |FRAME:CPD-15024 quercetin 3-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-gentiobioside-7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnoside|. The flavonol 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-rhamnosyltransferase was highly expressed in floral buds but showed only a low expression in roots, stems and siliques. Whereas the |FRAME:CPD1F-90 kaempferol| glycosides dominated in leaves, |FRAME:CPD-520 quercetin| glycosides were more abundant in floral buds and flowers |CITS:[17314094]|.</Comment>
